.TH diff "" "Command"
.SH NAME
diff \- summarize differences between two files
.SH SYNOPSIS
\fBdiff [\-bdefhs] [\-c \fIsymbol\fB] \fIfile1 file2\fR
.SH DESCRIPTION
.HS
diff -- summarize differences between two files
Usage:	diff [ -bdefhs ] [ -c sym ] file1 file2
Options:
	-b	Ignore trailing blanks; all strings of blanks are equal
	-c sym	Produce C pre-processor input conditionalized on 'sym'
	-d	Use -h algorithm for larger (>25,000 character) files
	-e	Produce editor script
	-f	Produce fake (non-usable) editor script
	-h	Half-hearted algorithm (works on long files)
	-s	Produce sed script
If either 'file1' or 'file2' is '-', the standard input is used.
If one of the files is a directory, then the other file name
under that directory is used.
.HE
.B diff
compares
.I file1
against
.I file2
and, by default, prints a script describing the changes needed to
turn
.I file1
into
.IR file2 .
Each change is introduced by a line of the form
.I "l1,l2" \fBa\fR\^, \fBc\fR\^, or \fBd\fR\ \fIl3,l4\fR
(append, change, or delete), where the line-number ranges, abbreviated
to a single number when a range covers only one line, refer to
.I file1
and
.I file2
respectively.
The text removed from
.I file1
follows, each line marked with \fB<\ \fR, then a \fB\-\-\-\fR
separator, then the text added from
.IR file2 ,
each line marked with \fB>\ \fR.
.PP
If either
.I file1
or
.I file2
is
.BR \- ,
the standard input is read for it.
If one of the two names a directory, the file compared from that
directory is the one whose base name matches the other argument.
Either input may be a special file or a pipe; such input is copied to
a temporary file under
.B /tmp
before comparison, since it cannot otherwise be re-read.
.IP \fB\-b\fR 0.6i
Ignore trailing blanks, and treat any run of blanks (spaces and tabs)
within a line as equivalent to any other run of blanks, when deciding
whether two lines are equal.
.IP "\fB\-c \fIsymbol\fR"
In place of the default script, print the merged text of both files
as input to the C preprocessor.
A range found only in
.I file1
(deleted from
.IR file2 )
is wrapped in \fB#ifdef \fIsymbol\fR ... \fB#endif\fR, so it survives
preprocessing only when
.I symbol
is defined.
A range found only in
.I file2
(appended) is wrapped in \fB#ifndef \fIsymbol\fR ... \fB#endif\fR, so
it survives only when
.I symbol
is
.I not
defined.
A changed range is wrapped as \fB#ifndef \fIsymbol\fR, then the old
.I file1
text, then \fB#else\fR, then the new
.I file2
text, then \fB#endif\fR -- note that this puts the old text under
the
.I "undefined"
branch and the new text under the
.I defined
branch, the reverse of the polarity used for a pure deletion or
append (see NOTES).
Cannot be combined with
.BR \-e .
.IP \fB\-d\fR
If either input file is larger than 25,000 bytes, silently switch to
the algorithm used by
.BR \-h ,
instead of the default one.
.IP \fB\-e\fR
Produce a script of
.B ed
commands that, applied to
.I file1
with line numbers adjusted for each preceding edit, turns it into
.IR file2 .
.IP \fB\-f\fR
Like
.BR \-e ,
but the line numbers in the script are not adjusted for the effect of
earlier entries in the same script; the script must therefore be
applied from its last entry to its first, and is not directly usable
by
.BR ed .
.IP \fB\-h\fR
Compare the files using an algorithm that requires only a small,
constant amount of working storage per matched region, at the cost of
occasionally missing the shortest possible list of differences; it
does not have the practical file-size limit that the default
algorithm has.
This option execs
.BR /usr/lib/diffh ,
a separate binary built from the same driver code.
.IP \fB\-s\fR
Like
.BR \-e ,
but produce a
.B sed
script instead of an
.B ed
script, and, at the end, print a line of the form
.B :I\fIn\fB
.B :D\fIn\fB
.B :U\fIn\fR
giving the number of lines inserted, deleted, and left unchanged.
.SH FILES
.DS
.ta 1.6i
\fB/tmp/difXXXXXX\fR	temporary copy of a special file or pipe input
\fB/usr/lib/diffh\fR	the half-hearted algorithm, invoked by \-h and \-d
.DE
.SH "SEE ALSO"
.BR diff3 ,
.BR cmp ,
.B ed
.SH DIAGNOSTICS
.B diff
exits with status 0 if the files are identical, 1 if they differ, and
2 if it could not open a file or was given bad arguments.
.SH NOTES
.B \-c
and
.B \-e
cannot be given together.
.PP
With
.BR \-c ,
a pure deletion or a pure append uses whichever of
.B #ifdef
or
.B #ifndef
lets it write a single, non-empty conditional block; a changed range
always uses \fB#ifndef \fIsymbol\fR/\fB#else\fR, with the old text
first.
As a result, the polarity of
.I symbol
needed to reconstruct
.I file1
is not the same for a changed range as it is for a purely deleted
range; check the generated output before relying on it.
